1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,080
Same vial.

2
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:02,239
Same peptide.

3
00:00:02,239 --> 00:00:03,541
Two different clocks.

4
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:05,455
The only difference is temperature.

5
00:00:05,455 --> 00:00:13,151
In water, the chemistry that breaks a peptide down is still running: hydrolysis, deamidation, oxidation.

6
00:00:13,151 --> 00:00:15,052
Heat speeds that chemistry up.

7
00:00:15,052 --> 00:00:21,927
A common pharmaceutical rule of thumb says some reaction rates roughly double for every ten degrees Celsius warmer.

8
00:00:21,927 --> 00:00:25,039
It is a rule of thumb, not a universal law.

9
00:00:25,039 --> 00:00:27,669
But it explains why cold slows the clock.

10
00:00:27,669 --> 00:00:30,429
Room temperature is about twenty-five degrees.

11
00:00:30,429 --> 00:00:32,096
A fridge is two to eight.

12
00:00:32,096 --> 00:00:36,796
That gap can mean the chemistry clock runs several times slower in the cold.

13
00:00:36,796 --> 00:00:38,033
The labels agree.

14
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:44,479
Ozempic's label says a new pen goes in the refrigerator, two to eight degrees, and never in the freezer.

15
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:46,640
Cold slows the chemistry clock.

16
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:47,929
It does not stop it.

17
00:00:47,929 --> 00:00:49,309
Day five of ten.

18
00:00:49,309 --> 00:00:51,419
Follow so you don't miss day six.

